  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA cold surface ridge of high pressure dominates the local and \nregional weather across the central US this morning as temperatures \nhave slowly made their way into the low 40s by midday today. Under \nbroken decks of cirrus and light diurnal mixing, temperatures will \nnot warm too much further this afternoon, topping out in the upper \n40s. By Sunday, the shortwave that currently resides over the central\nRockies will shift across Kansas and Oklahoma bringing rain chances \nto areas south of the forecast area. Similar temperatures can be \nexpected Sunday given the mostly cloudy skies as highs top out in the\nlow 50s.\n\nThe early portion of the week will be characterized by a deepening \nridge over the central US with southerly flow returning better \nmoisture and warmer temperatures to northeastern Kansas. Expect \ntemperatures to warm each afternoon through the week with highs in \nthe upper 60s by Friday. A weak cold front is progged to slide \nacross the area Wednesday evening that may yield some \nisolated/scattered showers over eastern Kansas (15-25% chances). \nThat said, the best and most widespread chances for precipitation in \nthe next week will come Friday into Saturday (60-80%) as a larger \ntrough ejects off the central Rockies with better surface moisture \nin place. Temperature spreads following the passage of the weekend\ntrough trend downward as a cooler airmass from the north builds south\nacross Kansas into the early following week.",
